## Database Models

### 1. User Model
The User model stores library user information.
Fields include:
- id
- name
- email
- password
- role
- createdAt
- updatedAt

The Role enum defines two user categories:

- PATRON: Regular library users.
- LIBRARIAN: Users responsible for managing library operations.

### 2. Genre Model

The Genre model stores book categories.

Fields include:
- id
- name
- description
- createdAt
- updatedAt

Each genre can contain multiple books.

### 3. Book Model
The Book model stores information about library books.
Fields include:
- id
- title
- author
- isbn
- description
- genreId
- createdAt
- updatedAt

The genreId field acts as a foreign key referencing the
Genre model.

## Schema Decisions
## Why Book References Genre Instead of Embedding It
The Book model references the Genre model using a foreign
key called genreId rather than embedding genre information
directly inside every book record.

This design follows relational database normalization
principles.
The decision provides several advantages:
1. Data Consistency:
   Each book references an existing genre, ensuring that
   genre information remains consistent across the database.

2. Reduced Data Duplication:
   Genre names and descriptions are stored once in the
   Genre table instead of being repeated in every book record.

3. Easier Maintenance:
   Updating a genre name or description does not require
   updating every associated book.

4. Referential Integrity:
   Prisma uses the @relation attribute to establish the
   relationship between Book and Genre through genreId.

5. Scalability:
   New genres and books can be added independently without
   changing the database structure.

6. Efficient Data Retrieval:
   Prisma supports relational queries using include and
   select to retrieve books together with their genres.

### Relationship Structure
One Genre can have many Books.
Each Book belongs to one Genre.

The relationship is implemented using:
- Genre.books: Book[]
- Book.genreId: Int
- Book.genre: Genre @relation(fields: [genreId], references: [id])

This represents a One-to-Many relationship.
## Database Seeding

The seed.ts file inserts:
- 4 Genres
- 15 Books

The books are distributed across Fiction, Science,
Technology, and History genres.
The seeding process uses Prisma upsert() to prevent
duplicate records when executed repeatedly.

## Database Configuration
Create a .env file in the api directory.

Configure your PostgreSQL connection:
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:PORT/DATABASE?schema=public"

## Installation and Setup
Install dependencies:
npm install
Generate Prisma Client:
npx prisma generate
Run database migration:
npx prisma migrate dev --name init
Run database seeding:
npx ts-node prisma/seed.ts
Open Prisma Studio:
npx prisma studio
## Conclusion

The database uses a normalized relational structure
with clear relationships between users, genres, and books.
Prisma ORM provides database connectivity, schema
management, and type-safe database operations.
